# Clean Architecture for gRPC Server

## Project Structure Overview
Based on [cmd/main.go](mdc:cmd/main.go) and [cmd/commands/start.go](mdc:cmd/commands/start.go), follow this layered architecture:

```
gRPC-server/
├── cmd/                    # Application entry points
├── internal/
│   ├── domain/            # Business entities (innermost layer)
│   ├── usecase/           # Business logic / application services
│   ├── repository/        # Data access interfaces
│   ├── handler/           # gRPC handlers (adapters)
│   ├── infrastructure/    # External dependencies
│   └── config/           # Configuration
├── pkg/                   # Public libraries
└── proto/                # Protocol buffer definitions
```

## Layer Dependencies (Dependency Rule)
- **Domain** ← **UseCase** ← **Handler** ← **Infrastructure**
- Inner layers should never depend on outer layers
- Use dependency inversion to break dependencies

## Domain Layer (Core Business Logic)
- Contains business entities and rules
- No dependencies on external frameworks
- Pure Go structs and interfaces

```go
// internal/domain/user.go
type User struct {
    ID    string
    Name  string
    Email string
}

type UserRepository interface {
    Save(ctx context.Context, user *User) error
    FindByID(ctx context.Context, id string) (*User, error)
}
```

## UseCase Layer (Application Services)
- Orchestrates business operations
- Depends only on domain layer
- Contains application-specific logic

```go
// internal/usecase/user.go
type UserUseCase struct {
    repo UserRepository
    validator UserValidator
}

func (uc *UserUseCase) CreateUser(ctx context.Context, user *User) error {
    if err := uc.validator.Validate(user); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    return uc.repo.Save(ctx, user)
}
```

## Handler Layer (Interface Adapters)
- Converts between gRPC and domain models
- Handles protocol-specific concerns
- References the protobuf from [proto/user.proto](mdc:proto/user.proto)

```go
// internal/handler/user_handler.go
type UserHandler struct {
    usecase UserUseCase
    adapter UserAdapter
}

func (h *UserHandler) CreateUser(ctx context.Context, req *pb.CreateUserRequest) (*pb.CreateUserResponse, error) {
    user := h.adapter.FromProto(req.User)
    err := h.usecase.CreateUser(ctx, user)
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    return &pb.CreateUserResponse{User: h.adapter.ToProto(user)}, nil
}
```

## Infrastructure Layer (External Dependencies)
- Database implementations
- External service clients
- Framework-specific code

```go
// internal/infrastructure/repository/memory_user_repo.go
type MemoryUserRepository struct {
    users map[string]*domain.User
    mutex sync.RWMutex
}

func (r *MemoryUserRepository) Save(ctx context.Context, user *domain.User) error {
    r.mutex.Lock()
    defer r.mutex.Unlock()
    r.users[user.ID] = user
    return nil
}
```

## Configuration Layer
- Environment-specific settings
- Dependency injection setup

```go
// internal/config/config.go
type Config struct {
    Server ServerConfig
    Database DatabaseConfig
}

type Container struct {
    Config *Config
    UserUseCase *usecase.UserUseCase
    UserHandler *handler.UserHandler
}
```

## Integration with Cobra CLI
Extend [cmd/commands/start.go](mdc:cmd/commands/start.go) to use clean architecture:

```go
func runStart(cmd *cobra.Command, args []string) {
    // Load configuration
    cfg := config.Load()
    
    // Build dependency container
    container := config.NewContainer(cfg)
    
    // Create gRPC server with clean architecture
    server := grpc.NewServer()
    pb.RegisterUserServiceServer(server, container.UserHandler)
    
    // Start server...
}
```

## Key Principles

### 1. Dependency Inversion
- High-level modules don't depend on low-level modules
- Both depend on abstractions (interfaces)

### 2. Separation of Concerns
- Each layer has a single responsibility
- Business logic isolated from technical concerns

### 3. Testability
- Easy to mock dependencies
- Unit test each layer independently

### 4. Framework Independence
- Core business logic doesn't depend on gRPC, databases, etc.
- Can swap implementations without changing business rules

### 5. Interface Segregation
- Small, focused interfaces
- Clients depend only on methods they use

## Testing Strategy
```go
// Test usecase without infrastructure dependencies
func TestUserUseCase_CreateUser(t *testing.T) {
    mockRepo := &MockUserRepository{}
    mockValidator := &MockUserValidator{}
    
    usecase := usecase.NewUserUseCase(mockRepo, mockValidator)
    
    // Test business logic in isolation
}
```
